<h2>Cardamom's most concentrated market cluster</h2>
<p>No other spice we export is as geographically concentrated as cardamom: the UAE and Saudi Arabia together account for roughly 61% of India's total cardamom export value, a level of concentration well beyond what we see for turmeric, cumin or red chilli. The UAE's own volume, combined with its role as a regional trading and re-export hub, makes it one of the most important single relationships in our entire spice portfolio despite cardamom being a comparatively low-weight, high-value product.</p>

<h2>High value changes how you think about a shipment</h2>
<p>Because cardamom loads by volume rather than weight — roughly 8–10 MT per 20ft container, well under what a bulk commodity like maize or onion carries — the cargo value per container is disproportionately high. That has practical implications: packaging integrity, insurance and storage conditions matter more here than on our higher-tonnage, lower-value lines, since a single container represents significantly more capital at risk in transit.</p>

<h2>Grade selection follows the buyer type</h2>
<p>Bold 8mm+ pods dominate premium Gulf retail and food-service demand and carry the highest price; AGEB, AGS and smaller commercial grades serve cost-conscious bulk buyers. The UAE market spans both ends of that range, so tell us your target buyer and price point rather than assuming one grade fits all Gulf demand.</p>

<h2>Fast transit, but packaging discipline still matters</h2>
<p>At 3–6 days to Jebel Ali, this is one of the shortest lanes we ship cardamom on, which limits (but doesn't eliminate) the aroma-loss risk that airtight, moisture-protected packaging is designed to prevent. Cardamom degrades faster than most spices when exposed to heat, light or air — worth confirming your packaging spec even on a short route like this one.</p>

